WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.635
Hello everyone. Thank you for
coming to this talk today.

00:00:06.636 --> 00:00:09.185
This is the final
intern talk by Andrea.

00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:11.790
Andrea has been an intern
with us for this summer on

00:00:11.791 --> 00:00:15.370
these special internship
projects on spatial audio.

00:00:15.371 --> 00:00:17.925
He is a PhD student at
New York University,

00:00:17.926 --> 00:00:19.345
and he will be talking about

00:00:19.346 --> 00:00:21.220
Blind Room Volume Estimation.

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:22.685
With that, Andrea please.

00:00:22.686 --> 00:00:25.830
&gt;&gt; Thank you Anis.
All right thanks

00:00:25.831 --> 00:00:27.520
for coming to my talk.

00:00:27.521 --> 00:00:29.730
The title for my talk is Towards

00:00:29.731 --> 00:00:32.330
Real-time Blind Room
Volume Estimation from

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:35.860
Single Channel Audio Signals
in Noisy Conditions.

00:00:35.861 --> 00:00:39.140
The word "Towards"
here is important.

00:00:39.141 --> 00:00:42.705
So, let's move to the outline.

00:00:42.706 --> 00:00:45.320
So, today I'm going to
give some background and

00:00:45.321 --> 00:00:47.105
motivation of why this project

00:00:47.106 --> 00:00:49.130
was fought in the first place.

00:00:49.131 --> 00:00:51.620
Some previous work and

00:00:51.621 --> 00:00:54.515
objectives that we
derived from literature.

00:00:54.516 --> 00:00:56.930
Our proposed approach,
and we're going to go

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:59.759
through the data
that we collected,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.160
the features that we
decided to compute,

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:05.165
our prediction model,
and the results.

00:01:05.166 --> 00:01:06.845
Then finally, we're
going to discuss

00:01:06.846 --> 00:01:08.150
some future works and

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:10.645
improvements that
could be implemented,

00:01:10.646 --> 00:01:13.295
some conclusions and discussion.

00:01:13.296 --> 00:01:17.250
So, let's start from
the background.

00:01:17.251 --> 00:01:21.870
So acoustic parameters,
why do we care about them.

00:01:21.871 --> 00:01:23.990
In mixed and augmented reality,

00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:26.870
virtual sources are desired to be

00:01:26.871 --> 00:01:28.580
perceived as fitting with

00:01:28.581 --> 00:01:31.040
the rest of the user
physical space.

00:01:31.041 --> 00:01:33.920
So, we want to mix
reality, real sources,

00:01:33.921 --> 00:01:35.240
and virtual sources to blend

00:01:35.241 --> 00:01:38.015
together in the same
perceptual space.

00:01:38.016 --> 00:01:40.830
So this would improve
auditory plausibility,

00:01:40.831 --> 00:01:44.330
spatial audio immersion,
spatial audio quality.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.470
So acoustic room tailoring
can be achieved by using

00:01:48.471 --> 00:01:50.060
a room impulse response

00:01:50.061 --> 00:01:52.010
that is specific to
the spacing questions.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.929
So, we can measure
room impulse responses

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.370
and that will describe,

00:01:55.371 --> 00:01:57.680
how does the sound behave for

00:01:57.681 --> 00:02:01.160
a particular source receiver
position is inside the room.

00:02:01.161 --> 00:02:02.810
As you can see from the image,

00:02:02.811 --> 00:02:04.445
this is more or less the idea

00:02:04.446 --> 00:02:06.920
that mixed reality
wants to achieve.

00:02:06.921 --> 00:02:08.240
So, you have a listener

00:02:08.241 --> 00:02:10.055
inside an environment
and you want

00:02:10.056 --> 00:02:11.390
the virtual sound source

00:02:11.391 --> 00:02:14.280
to blend together with
a real sound source.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.180
However, room impulse responses

00:02:17.181 --> 00:02:18.300
are very difficult to measure,

00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:20.220
they required
technical expertise.

00:02:20.221 --> 00:02:23.495
So, scientists have developed

00:02:23.496 --> 00:02:26.285
synthesis methods they can use

00:02:26.286 --> 00:02:29.890
acoustic parameters to
model an impulse response.

00:02:29.891 --> 00:02:36.080
So, as you can see
from this image,

00:02:36.081 --> 00:02:37.970
I think most of you are familiar,

00:02:37.971 --> 00:02:40.250
but I'm going to just
give a brief overview.

00:02:40.251 --> 00:02:41.850
There are impulse
responses made over

00:02:41.851 --> 00:02:45.970
the fuse part which is
independent of the location,

00:02:45.971 --> 00:02:48.840
the response is
taking within a room.

00:02:48.841 --> 00:02:50.925
But the first part, there is

00:02:50.926 --> 00:02:52.340
direct sound and
early reflection,

00:02:52.341 --> 00:02:55.020
that part is position dependent.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:02.975
So, going to the use
case of mixed reality,

00:03:02.976 --> 00:03:06.935
we may want to create

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:09.545
an adaptive algorithm that can

00:03:09.546 --> 00:03:11.480
dynamically adapt to the position

00:03:11.481 --> 00:03:13.290
of the user within a space.

00:03:13.291 --> 00:03:15.630
So, here we can see
for example a school,

00:03:15.631 --> 00:03:18.170
and there's some
HoloLens users that

00:03:18.171 --> 00:03:20.780
wants to move around
from an auditorium,

00:03:20.781 --> 00:03:21.790
he moves to a corridor,

00:03:21.791 --> 00:03:23.170
then he moves to a room,

00:03:23.171 --> 00:03:26.030
moves back to an auditorium
and goes around the building.

00:03:26.031 --> 00:03:27.410
Each one of these spaces,

00:03:27.411 --> 00:03:29.840
they sound very
different acoustically.

00:03:29.841 --> 00:03:33.230
So it is necessary to
capture the parameters

00:03:33.231 --> 00:03:35.150
that describe the local
acoustic environment

00:03:35.151 --> 00:03:37.530
in each one of
these spaces separately.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.200
The challenges of this problem is

00:03:42.201 --> 00:03:45.890
that blind estimations
cannot make

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:50.750
assumptions over
the local environment

00:03:50.751 --> 00:03:53.970
where somebody is
present at the time.

00:03:53.971 --> 00:03:57.020
So, for example, in this
image we can see many people

00:03:57.021 --> 00:04:00.330
wearing some sort of
mixed reality device,

00:04:00.331 --> 00:04:03.025
and they're surrounded
by noise sources.

00:04:03.026 --> 00:04:05.790
There is bubble nodes,
there is ambient noise,

00:04:05.791 --> 00:04:09.130
perhaps there's also a fan noise.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.929
They might be
all different devices

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.440
where some of them are
multiple microphones,

00:04:14.441 --> 00:04:16.225
some of them only
have one microphone,

00:04:16.226 --> 00:04:18.169
maybe some of them have cameras.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.080
So we wanted to think about
something which is more

00:04:21.081 --> 00:04:23.600
generalizable and simple for each

00:04:23.601 --> 00:04:26.530
one of these situations.

00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:29.870
So, our approach was to

00:04:29.871 --> 00:04:31.460
think of situations to make

00:04:31.461 --> 00:04:34.330
something that's
generalizable as possible.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.710
The way we decided to go is for

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:43.480
single channel
audio-based estimations.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.770
Blind estimation means we

00:04:46.771 --> 00:04:48.950
have no prior knowledge
of the space,

00:04:48.951 --> 00:04:50.600
we have no prior knowledge of

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:54.400
the sources around the listener,

00:04:54.401 --> 00:04:57.340
or the geometry of the room.

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:04.690
So, previous work on
acoustic parameters are mostly

00:05:04.691 --> 00:05:07.390
focused on two main parameters

00:05:07.391 --> 00:05:09.965
called the reverberation time,

00:05:09.966 --> 00:05:14.505
T60 or the DRR, the
Direct-to-Reverberant ratio.

00:05:14.506 --> 00:05:17.140
There's quite a big body
of literature

00:05:17.141 --> 00:05:21.170
about the blind prediction
of these two parameters.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:27.010
Other parameters have not been as

00:05:27.011 --> 00:05:30.825
covered as much as
the T60 and the DRR,

00:05:30.826 --> 00:05:33.850
but the T60 is basically
the time it takes for

00:05:33.851 --> 00:05:37.770
nucleus response to
decay down by 60dB,

00:05:37.771 --> 00:05:42.660
and the DRR is the
Direct-to-Reverberant ratio that

00:05:42.661 --> 00:05:45.360
describes the ratio between

00:05:45.361 --> 00:05:47.700
the first part and the late part.

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:51.545
More recently,
literature has proposed

00:05:51.546 --> 00:05:55.990
a different way to describe
an acoustic environment,

00:05:55.991 --> 00:05:58.685
and it does this by
the reverberation fingerprint.

00:05:58.686 --> 00:06:01.430
So, the reverberation
fingerprint is no harder than

00:06:01.431 --> 00:06:05.610
a frequency-dependent T60 decay

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:09.375
coupled with an initial
power spectrum,

00:06:09.376 --> 00:06:11.610
and the room volume.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:18.200
So at the beginning
of this internship,

00:06:18.201 --> 00:06:22.310
we started looking at T60 and
DRR, thinking okay, well,

00:06:22.311 --> 00:06:26.750
maybe we can improve what
has been done in the past.

00:06:26.751 --> 00:06:32.520
So, these are the results of
the A's Challenge in 2015.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.875
Back at the time,

00:06:35.876 --> 00:06:38.155
most methods were signal-based.

00:06:38.156 --> 00:06:40.675
There was not much

00:06:40.676 --> 00:06:45.855
machine-learning into
spatial audio at the time.

00:06:45.856 --> 00:06:47.660
So the best results were

00:06:47.661 --> 00:06:51.090
achieved by single
processing methods.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:58.260
After 2015, the numbers don't
really matter specifically,

00:06:58.261 --> 00:07:01.559
but basically octave

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:07.570
spectral-band based algorithms
were the best-performing.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.604
Recently, machine
learning approaches

00:07:12.605 --> 00:07:15.190
have improved
this emission of T60.

00:07:15.191 --> 00:07:17.030
So, these are the
results from two papers,

00:07:17.031 --> 00:07:19.850
one is from Hannes and

00:07:19.851 --> 00:07:23.275
another one is another paper
that came out this year.

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:25.620
These are two approaches

00:07:25.621 --> 00:07:28.460
that use machine learning
to predict the T60,

00:07:28.461 --> 00:07:30.005
and they show that

00:07:30.006 --> 00:07:31.430
quite an improvement from

00:07:31.431 --> 00:07:33.125
previous method can be achieved.

00:07:33.126 --> 00:07:36.740
So, they both outperformed any
of the algorithms in 2015.

00:07:36.741 --> 00:07:39.060
So, this was quite
an interesting thing to find

00:07:39.061 --> 00:07:44.055
because these approaches are
quite new in spatial audio,

00:07:44.056 --> 00:07:48.530
and they are
the first three attempts

00:07:48.531 --> 00:07:50.060
but they seem to produce

00:07:50.061 --> 00:07:52.985
already considerable
improvements.

00:07:52.986 --> 00:07:57.570
So we kept this in mind
as we went forward.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:03.440
One thing to know that
we took out of this is

00:08:03.441 --> 00:08:07.575
also the T60 is
almost the sole problem.

00:08:07.576 --> 00:08:09.980
The current literature shows

00:08:09.981 --> 00:08:12.080
really good results and

00:08:12.081 --> 00:08:14.105
of course it's possible
to improve it,

00:08:14.106 --> 00:08:17.660
but we thought that

00:08:17.661 --> 00:08:20.765
there is space to try to
compute something else.

00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:24.570
The DRR, on the other hand,

00:08:24.571 --> 00:08:27.375
it's a parameter that is
source-position dependent.

00:08:27.376 --> 00:08:29.345
So, if you estimate the DRR,

00:08:29.346 --> 00:08:32.235
you can estimate it for
one particular source,

00:08:32.236 --> 00:08:35.270
but that doesn't tell you
much about other sources that

00:08:35.271 --> 00:08:39.035
you want to render within
the local environment.

00:08:39.036 --> 00:08:41.885
So, instead we decided

00:08:41.886 --> 00:08:46.780
to choose a different parameter
and that was room volume.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.490
So why room volume?

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:54.070
Room volume as a parameter,

00:08:54.071 --> 00:08:55.190
as an acoustic parameter,

00:08:55.191 --> 00:08:59.000
can serve as a proxy to
obtain other parameters.

00:08:59.001 --> 00:09:00.480
So, for example,

00:09:00.481 --> 00:09:03.800
the critical distance can

00:09:03.801 --> 00:09:07.830
be described by a formula
that includes volume.

00:09:07.831 --> 00:09:10.955
Also the initial power
spectrum that we saw

00:09:10.956 --> 00:09:13.720
from the energy decay release,

00:09:13.721 --> 00:09:16.145
which was the T62 decay,

00:09:16.146 --> 00:09:19.610
the initial power spectrum
can be adapted using

00:09:19.611 --> 00:09:24.035
a ratio of a reference
volume and a local volume.

00:09:24.036 --> 00:09:26.210
That work has been also discussed

00:09:26.211 --> 00:09:27.390
during the AVA conference

00:09:27.391 --> 00:09:31.670
for those of you that were there.

00:09:31.671 --> 00:09:34.220
So it seems to be
something that right

00:09:34.221 --> 00:09:36.355
now people are starting
to think about,

00:09:36.356 --> 00:09:38.480
but there is
very little amount of

00:09:38.481 --> 00:09:40.910
literature about
the prediction of room volume.

00:09:40.911 --> 00:09:43.310
So, we decided that this

00:09:43.311 --> 00:09:45.860
might be a good opportunity
to try something new,

00:09:45.861 --> 00:09:50.420
and try to to see
what's possible to

00:09:50.421 --> 00:09:52.610
do using similar approaches

00:09:52.611 --> 00:09:56.080
that are being used
recently for T60.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.940
One question that we
had the beginning,

00:10:01.941 --> 00:10:04.150
is whether we can
hear volume at all.

00:10:04.151 --> 00:10:09.650
So, these audio examples,

00:10:09.651 --> 00:10:14.945
they show dry signal
convolved with

00:10:14.946 --> 00:10:17.765
free impulse responses from

00:10:17.766 --> 00:10:20.450
three different
rooms where the T60

00:10:20.451 --> 00:10:23.510
was the same but the volume
was very different.

00:10:23.511 --> 00:10:25.325
So, all the three examples,

00:10:25.326 --> 00:10:28.955
they have T60 time
of about one second,

00:10:28.956 --> 00:10:35.460
but the rooms where
they were recorded,

00:10:35.461 --> 00:10:36.870
they were all different sizes.

00:10:36.871 --> 00:10:40.400
So, the first room was
only 100 cubic meters,

00:10:40.401 --> 00:10:45.475
second was 900, and the third
was 8,000 cubic meters.

00:10:45.476 --> 00:10:47.850
So, I've tried to
play the signals.

00:10:47.851 --> 00:10:49.970
The problem is that they're
going to go through speakers,

00:10:49.971 --> 00:10:52.565
so we're going to hear
to volume this room

00:10:52.566 --> 00:10:56.040
on top of the volume of
the room it was recorded.

00:10:56.041 --> 00:10:58.730
This is the original signal

00:10:58.731 --> 00:11:02.740
which was provided
by Professor Purkey.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.030
This is the small room.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:13.775
This is the median room.

00:11:13.776 --> 00:11:16.680
This is the big room.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.210
Right. So, one of them,

00:11:19.211 --> 00:11:21.935
there's more room is clearly
different to their sound.

00:11:21.936 --> 00:11:26.870
Perhaps some absorption
coloration is involved there too.

00:11:26.871 --> 00:11:31.205
The medium and the
big, they're little.

00:11:31.206 --> 00:11:33.500
They sound more
similar in this space,

00:11:33.501 --> 00:11:35.140
but if you listen to them
through head phones,

00:11:35.141 --> 00:11:36.980
you can notice something

00:11:36.981 --> 00:11:38.685
different in the low frequencies.

00:11:38.686 --> 00:11:40.740
I'll just play them once more.

00:11:40.741 --> 00:11:45.675
So original, small room,

00:11:45.676 --> 00:11:53.080
medium room, and big
room. All right.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.945
So, that we don't know.

00:11:58.946 --> 00:12:00.505
I'm going to get to
that point later.

00:12:00.506 --> 00:12:02.740
&gt;&gt; Apparently not,
if you go back and

00:12:02.741 --> 00:12:05.405
see the form of
a critical distance.

00:12:05.406 --> 00:12:11.635
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's one of
the challenges. We don't know.

00:12:11.636 --> 00:12:15.110
The data that we collected is

00:12:15.111 --> 00:12:16.615
mostly from data sets who

00:12:16.616 --> 00:12:19.650
do not provide that information.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:25.620
So, this is one case example,

00:12:25.621 --> 00:12:27.660
but we'll produce more.

00:12:27.661 --> 00:12:29.730
&gt;&gt; Why is that, the
T60 is the same,

00:12:29.731 --> 00:12:32.600
so they have a similar
perceptual quality.

00:12:32.601 --> 00:12:33.940
The first thing you notice about

00:12:33.941 --> 00:12:35.685
the room is how
reverberant is it.

00:12:35.686 --> 00:12:37.340
They are similarly reverberant,

00:12:37.341 --> 00:12:39.510
but the volume is different.

00:12:39.511 --> 00:12:40.920
So, their properties are

00:12:40.921 --> 00:12:42.440
obviously different
because they have

00:12:42.441 --> 00:12:44.305
different volumes
but the same became.

00:12:44.306 --> 00:12:45.340
&gt;&gt; Fine.

00:12:45.341 --> 00:12:46.670
&gt;&gt; But the question is,

00:12:46.671 --> 00:12:48.360
can you hear a difference
just based on

00:12:48.361 --> 00:12:52.030
that one parameter changes,
which is the volume?

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:58.570
&gt;&gt; Right. So, just by
listening to these examples,

00:12:58.571 --> 00:13:03.899
we were thinking about
what possible representations

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.845
could capture this information,

00:13:05.846 --> 00:13:10.340
and we're going to get back
to these in later slides.

00:13:10.341 --> 00:13:16.880
So, previous work about
blind volume estimation.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:23.020
There's not as much literature
as 4060 or DRR.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.500
It's been treated
as a side problem,

00:13:26.501 --> 00:13:30.720
but recently, it's
been brought up again.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.810
In 2012, there was

00:13:33.811 --> 00:13:36.145
a paper about rooming
identification using

00:13:36.146 --> 00:13:43.660
Gaussian Mixture
Models and MFCCs.

00:13:43.661 --> 00:13:46.870
The problem with that paper
is that this use

00:13:46.871 --> 00:13:51.380
the same rooms in the training
and the test set.

00:13:51.381 --> 00:13:54.850
That as we can see later on,

00:13:54.851 --> 00:13:57.790
makes a big difference
in your results.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:00.775
They somehow achieve

00:14:00.776 --> 00:14:06.330
23 percent equal
error rate, I think.

00:14:06.331 --> 00:14:12.405
But the way they
divided floor room,

00:14:12.406 --> 00:14:16.265
sorry, impulse response
location within the room.

00:14:16.266 --> 00:14:19.015
But having the same
room in both sets,

00:14:19.016 --> 00:14:23.045
it's not really telling
you much, I think.

00:14:23.046 --> 00:14:25.260
&gt;&gt; What is GLMM?

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:33.315
&gt;&gt; I think Gaussian
Mixture Model.

00:14:33.316 --> 00:14:36.700
I think that is an M
there for some reason.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:40.270
I got to check that.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.220
Another paper by Shabtai

00:14:44.221 --> 00:14:48.720
used an abrupt speech
detector in speech signals,

00:14:48.721 --> 00:14:52.040
to detect the end of
a sentence of word,

00:14:52.041 --> 00:14:54.830
and from that moment in time,

00:14:54.831 --> 00:14:56.555
treat whatever comes after,

00:14:56.556 --> 00:14:58.685
as if it were an
impulse response.

00:14:58.686 --> 00:15:02.490
So, whenever a word was
uttered, the end of the word,

00:15:02.491 --> 00:15:06.365
especially after
a very sharp consonants

00:15:06.366 --> 00:15:09.365
could be treated as if that
was the impulse response.

00:15:09.366 --> 00:15:14.730
By selecting that time frame,

00:15:14.731 --> 00:15:18.410
they would try to
estimate the volume,

00:15:18.411 --> 00:15:23.330
also using Gaussian
Mixture Models.

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:30.135
But that paper only looked
at clean conditions,

00:15:30.136 --> 00:15:31.835
so free from noise.

00:15:31.836 --> 00:15:36.720
That matter was also brought
up again in a recent paper,

00:15:36.721 --> 00:15:40.215
where they implemented
the same method,

00:15:40.216 --> 00:15:43.790
and they trained it for
a regression model.

00:15:43.791 --> 00:15:47.770
But they only tested
this method on

00:15:47.771 --> 00:15:53.500
a ISM data, so
image-source model.

00:15:53.501 --> 00:15:55.090
So, the image source
model creates

00:15:55.091 --> 00:15:58.200
a very clean synthesize
impulse response,

00:15:58.201 --> 00:16:02.810
which works well for shoe box
rooms, rectangular rooms,

00:16:02.811 --> 00:16:03.830
but on the other hand,

00:16:03.831 --> 00:16:07.415
is not really representative
of real situations.

00:16:07.416 --> 00:16:09.200
So, even there, they

00:16:09.201 --> 00:16:12.380
trained something that works
in a theoretical model,

00:16:12.381 --> 00:16:17.380
but there was no test
on actual rules.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:24.270
So, this left quite
a big space for us to try to

00:16:24.271 --> 00:16:27.055
come up with a regression model

00:16:27.056 --> 00:16:28.370
that could try to estimate

00:16:28.371 --> 00:16:30.610
volume from anytime
situation. Yes.

00:16:30.611 --> 00:16:33.940
&gt;&gt; So, we have

00:16:33.941 --> 00:16:36.780
a source somewhere in
the room, and a microphone.

00:16:36.781 --> 00:16:39.125
We have no idea of
where the source is,

00:16:39.126 --> 00:16:40.305
where the microphone is, and we

00:16:40.306 --> 00:16:41.670
want to estimate the volume.

00:16:41.671 --> 00:16:43.260
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, that's

00:16:43.261 --> 00:16:46.180
the highest level of blindness
that you can achieve.

00:16:46.181 --> 00:16:47.640
You don't know where you are.

00:16:47.641 --> 00:16:49.000
You don't know where
the sources is.

00:16:49.001 --> 00:16:50.270
How many sources
there are? If there's

00:16:50.271 --> 00:16:51.990
noise, if there's no noise?

00:16:51.991 --> 00:16:53.925
We wanted to start with

00:16:53.926 --> 00:16:57.200
the least amount of
assumptions as possible.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:01.465
Then, of course,
future work and start

00:17:01.466 --> 00:17:03.370
making side assumptions and

00:17:03.371 --> 00:17:05.130
try to see semi-blind methods.

00:17:05.131 --> 00:17:07.320
We're going to get that, too.

00:17:07.321 --> 00:17:11.210
So, our proposed approach

00:17:11.211 --> 00:17:13.280
is to detect room volume blindly

00:17:13.281 --> 00:17:16.935
from signals using
a convolutional neural network

00:17:16.936 --> 00:17:18.720
and form a frame.

00:17:18.721 --> 00:17:21.440
The problem is a
regression problem.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:25.480
So, we aim to
simulate a variety of

00:17:25.481 --> 00:17:27.050
noisy environment containing

00:17:27.051 --> 00:17:29.850
reverberant speech
within the environment,

00:17:29.851 --> 00:17:33.505
and represent as much range
of room size as possible,

00:17:33.506 --> 00:17:37.230
and as much real impulse
response data as possible.

00:17:37.231 --> 00:17:42.010
So, their impulse response
are used to simulate,

00:17:42.011 --> 00:17:45.035
sorry, the noisy environments.

00:17:45.036 --> 00:17:48.980
So, we thought about
training a network

00:17:48.981 --> 00:17:52.580
from short single channel
audio recordings,

00:17:52.581 --> 00:17:54.355
so four seconds long,

00:17:54.356 --> 00:17:57.740
using different SNR levels.

00:17:57.741 --> 00:18:00.820
So, we simulated different ratios

00:18:00.821 --> 00:18:04.270
between the noise and the
speech, the reverberant speech.

00:18:04.271 --> 00:18:08.260
The main goal was to
evaluate the results,

00:18:08.261 --> 00:18:10.250
to gain insights
into the problem,

00:18:10.251 --> 00:18:12.800
and try to move
forward from there.

00:18:12.801 --> 00:18:15.750
The four second extract is

00:18:15.751 --> 00:18:18.700
also relevant for
real-time estimation.

00:18:18.701 --> 00:18:21.790
So, although it's not
exactly real time,

00:18:21.791 --> 00:18:26.480
a four second chunk of audio
is fairly fast to evaluate.

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:32.150
That also part of the
requirements so mixed reality,

00:18:32.151 --> 00:18:34.745
as somebody moves
around the building.

00:18:34.746 --> 00:18:37.940
So, this is the pipeline
that we set in place.

00:18:37.941 --> 00:18:39.580
I'm going to talk
about each one of

00:18:39.581 --> 00:18:41.930
these steps: we the
collected data;

00:18:41.931 --> 00:18:44.985
we synthesize also some data;

00:18:44.986 --> 00:18:49.280
then we went on with
the data augmentation;

00:18:49.281 --> 00:18:51.285
simulated the noisy rooms;

00:18:51.286 --> 00:18:53.550
made our sets, our training,

00:18:53.551 --> 00:18:57.315
and development, and
evaluations sets;

00:18:57.316 --> 00:18:59.650
we proceed to feature extraction;

00:18:59.651 --> 00:19:02.800
we train model; we
evaluated the model;

00:19:02.801 --> 00:19:05.380
and then that would inform us on

00:19:05.381 --> 00:19:09.070
how did the feature
perform in the model,

00:19:09.071 --> 00:19:11.730
and then go back to
extract more features,

00:19:11.731 --> 00:19:13.980
or change some parameters.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.750
All right, so data collection.

00:19:19.751 --> 00:19:20.900
This was definitely

00:19:20.901 --> 00:19:23.380
the most time-consuming part
of the internship,

00:19:23.381 --> 00:19:26.000
where several weeks
were spent into

00:19:26.001 --> 00:19:29.440
retrieving online data sets,

00:19:29.441 --> 00:19:31.600
public data sets, and format them

00:19:31.601 --> 00:19:34.395
into something that could
be given to a network.

00:19:34.396 --> 00:19:39.500
So, we found 11 public room
impulse response data sets

00:19:39.501 --> 00:19:41.710
and two internal data sets.

00:19:41.711 --> 00:19:44.365
We only were interested in rooms

00:19:44.366 --> 00:19:47.085
for which we add room
volume information.

00:19:47.086 --> 00:19:49.940
So, this proved a challenge,

00:19:49.941 --> 00:19:53.280
just the time-consuming
challenge to prune each data

00:19:53.281 --> 00:19:57.300
set from the entries which did
not have that information.

00:19:57.301 --> 00:19:59.690
We had to do some custom
information retrieval

00:19:59.691 --> 00:20:04.185
to associate that
information correctly,

00:20:04.186 --> 00:20:05.865
clean up the inconsistencies,

00:20:05.866 --> 00:20:08.055
parse into a single file.

00:20:08.056 --> 00:20:11.955
Boring work but time-consuming.

00:20:11.956 --> 00:20:17.370
But at the end of
the data collection,

00:20:17.371 --> 00:20:20.925
we ended up with 84 unique rooms.

00:20:20.926 --> 00:20:23.890
As you can see, they all
come from different places,

00:20:23.891 --> 00:20:27.780
and they were sampled at
different sampling frequencies.

00:20:27.781 --> 00:20:29.595
For some reason, one shows

00:20:29.596 --> 00:20:35.010
a sampling frequency
of 12.780 kilohertz.

00:20:35.011 --> 00:20:36.870
It's kind of odd,

00:20:36.871 --> 00:20:39.045
but it's from 1993.

00:20:39.046 --> 00:20:40.470
It's a old data set,

00:20:40.471 --> 00:20:45.670
so maybe the standards
were not as set as now.

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:50.540
We resampled all this data,
216 kilohertz,

00:20:50.541 --> 00:20:55.730
I believe, which is good
enough for speech signals.

00:20:55.731 --> 00:20:57.880
So, these are some stats about

00:20:57.881 --> 00:21:00.810
the rules that we obtained.

00:21:00.811 --> 00:21:04.180
So, the first graph shows
how many unique rooms per

00:21:04.181 --> 00:21:08.485
dataset we were able to extract.

00:21:08.486 --> 00:21:11.950
The second plot shows

00:21:11.951 --> 00:21:15.300
the room volume distribution
on a logarithmic scale.

00:21:15.301 --> 00:21:17.245
So, the logarithmic scale,

00:21:17.246 --> 00:21:20.910
once we represent
the fact that differences

00:21:20.911 --> 00:21:25.910
between small rooms should
be treated differently,

00:21:25.911 --> 00:21:30.005
that the same difference
in a larger scale.

00:21:30.006 --> 00:21:36.250
So, for example, a room that
is 20 cubic meters can be

00:21:36.251 --> 00:21:39.460
sound very different from room
that is 40 cubic meters by

00:21:39.461 --> 00:21:42.965
a room that is
2,020 cubic meters,

00:21:42.966 --> 00:21:44.780
probably does not sound
very different than

00:21:44.781 --> 00:21:47.275
a room that is
2,040 cubic meters.

00:21:47.276 --> 00:21:49.330
So, that's why we're representing

00:21:49.331 --> 00:21:53.110
the distribution on
a logarithmic scale.

00:21:53.111 --> 00:21:56.510
As you can see,
the main problem there

00:21:56.511 --> 00:21:59.085
is that we add
a gap in the middle

00:21:59.086 --> 00:22:05.400
about available room volumes
in our dataset,

00:22:05.401 --> 00:22:11.020
which we fixed a little bit
through data simulation,

00:22:11.021 --> 00:22:13.600
and tried to later on, although

00:22:13.601 --> 00:22:16.100
a part of the gap still remained.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.735
The last graph on the right,

00:22:19.736 --> 00:22:26.740
is just a histogram of the
repetitions or takes per room.

00:22:26.741 --> 00:22:32.370
So, each one of these datasets
took different,

00:22:32.371 --> 00:22:34.030
they're all different in terms

00:22:34.031 --> 00:22:36.740
of how many positions
did they record,

00:22:36.741 --> 00:22:41.605
what speakers are
keeping did they use.

00:22:41.606 --> 00:22:46.160
Some datasets have maybe

00:22:46.161 --> 00:22:48.810
hundreds of takes
inside a single room,

00:22:48.811 --> 00:22:52.025
and some are dataset are
only one take inside a room.

00:22:52.026 --> 00:22:53.635
So, as a result

00:22:53.636 --> 00:22:55.410
the final distribution of

00:22:55.411 --> 00:22:57.405
how many input response we have,

00:22:57.406 --> 00:23:00.880
is plotted on the graph
on the right.

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:07.315
There is quite a bias towards
these either small rooms,

00:23:07.316 --> 00:23:10.920
office sides kind of
room or very large rooms

00:23:10.921 --> 00:23:16.825
because quite a few concert halls

00:23:16.826 --> 00:23:19.410
in some of the datasets.

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:26.035
Moving on. Then, we decided

00:23:26.036 --> 00:23:33.470
to cap the representation to
50 takes per unique room.

00:23:33.471 --> 00:23:35.210
So, each unique room was

00:23:35.211 --> 00:23:37.940
represented a maximum
of 50 times.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:46.690
So, simulated data, so Anis
managed together with Nikush

00:23:46.691 --> 00:23:54.025
to simulate 50 rooms using
randomly created polygons,

00:23:54.026 --> 00:23:57.590
which were shaped in

00:23:57.591 --> 00:24:00.760
a way to avoid very
regular rectangular rooms.

00:24:00.761 --> 00:24:03.350
So, these irregular polygons

00:24:03.351 --> 00:24:06.000
create more realistic geometries,

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:11.335
and the simulation was set up for

00:24:11.336 --> 00:24:17.845
100 random receiver position
inside this model,

00:24:17.846 --> 00:24:20.230
and Triton was used to simulate

00:24:20.231 --> 00:24:23.230
impulse responses for a range of

00:24:23.231 --> 00:24:28.660
volumes from 60 to more or
less 3,000 cubic meters.

00:24:28.661 --> 00:24:31.720
The results of these models

00:24:31.721 --> 00:24:34.020
are band limited
to two kilohertz,

00:24:34.021 --> 00:24:37.010
due to the way Triton
works right now.

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:43.335
This data was used to
make a parallel dataset

00:24:43.336 --> 00:24:45.880
where we could compare
the effect of adding

00:24:45.881 --> 00:24:48.640
simulator rooms against not
having simulator rooms.

00:24:48.641 --> 00:24:49.445
&gt;&gt; In train.

00:24:49.446 --> 00:24:56.590
&gt;&gt; In train. Yes.
No simulated data

00:24:56.591 --> 00:25:00.815
was put into the test
set, or in the train set.

00:25:00.816 --> 00:25:05.060
Right. So, once our data
collection was completed,

00:25:05.061 --> 00:25:07.880
we moved on to
the corpus creation.

00:25:07.881 --> 00:25:11.410
So, this is, if you're familiar
with the ACE challenge,

00:25:11.411 --> 00:25:16.965
this is pretty much
the same setup where

00:25:16.966 --> 00:25:20.760
room impulse response
two roomy push responses

00:25:20.761 --> 00:25:23.790
belonging to the same
room were taken,

00:25:23.791 --> 00:25:25.130
and one was convolved with

00:25:25.131 --> 00:25:27.625
a speech extract,
randomly chosen,

00:25:27.626 --> 00:25:30.030
and the other one was convolved

00:25:30.031 --> 00:25:32.450
with either babble noise
or ambit noise.

00:25:32.451 --> 00:25:39.235
An SNR was chosen between
four different levels, zero dB,

00:25:39.236 --> 00:25:42.495
plus 10 dB, plus
20 dB, an infinite,

00:25:42.496 --> 00:25:47.580
meaning that no noise was
included in some examples.

00:25:47.581 --> 00:25:49.925
The signals were added together,

00:25:49.926 --> 00:25:53.365
and then it was split
into four-second windows.

00:25:53.366 --> 00:25:56.630
&gt;&gt; There's still the
speech corpus, you use it,

00:25:56.631 --> 00:25:59.855
[inaudible] the circleness
and that precludes speech.

00:25:59.856 --> 00:26:02.260
There are 40s to 60 degrees,

00:26:02.261 --> 00:26:04.030
60 is the studio recordings.

00:26:04.031 --> 00:26:07.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Yes. So, infinite is

00:26:07.371 --> 00:26:11.285
actually in relationship
to the noise that we add,

00:26:11.286 --> 00:26:14.400
not to the noise that was
already in the speech actually.

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.145
&gt;&gt; Why did you convolve

00:26:18.146 --> 00:26:23.410
the noise with
the room pulse response.

00:26:23.411 --> 00:26:27.330
&gt;&gt; So, to make

00:26:27.331 --> 00:26:31.810
the noise belong to the same
room as the speech signal.

00:26:31.811 --> 00:26:35.215
&gt;&gt; Let me ask, just the
signal direction and noise

00:26:35.216 --> 00:26:38.890
was in the room
[inaudible] weird.

00:26:38.891 --> 00:26:42.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's like you
place loudspeaker in a room,

00:26:42.031 --> 00:26:44.130
then you play that noise.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:50.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I guess it's a way

00:26:50.821 --> 00:26:55.210
to just setup the problem

00:26:55.211 --> 00:26:58.915
from a single
channel perspective,

00:26:58.916 --> 00:27:00.930
yes, I you see what you mean,

00:27:00.931 --> 00:27:04.640
but having a, yeah.

00:27:04.641 --> 00:27:06.280
In the end everything
was single channels,

00:27:06.281 --> 00:27:14.105
so it's to try to come up
with a simple model for now.

00:27:14.106 --> 00:27:18.535
&gt;&gt; I guess one aspect,
which is a little bit,

00:27:18.536 --> 00:27:21.990
a very little bit deeper in
the ACE challenge setup is

00:27:21.991 --> 00:27:25.670
that the ACE challenge
was recorded.

00:27:25.671 --> 00:27:28.370
On Ace challenge
the corpus must generate

00:27:28.371 --> 00:27:31.275
by recording actual noise
in a real room,

00:27:31.276 --> 00:27:34.300
and then mixing it into signal.

00:27:34.301 --> 00:27:36.280
What we were trying
to do is generate

00:27:36.281 --> 00:27:38.850
training data that would
simulate that somewhat,

00:27:38.851 --> 00:27:40.920
but the ambient noise that was in

00:27:40.921 --> 00:27:45.425
this real recordings isn't
just some fan noise somewhere.

00:27:45.426 --> 00:27:47.030
When I listened to it at least,

00:27:47.031 --> 00:27:48.780
I heard that there is
somebody walking down

00:27:48.781 --> 00:27:50.790
the corridor because
this was a public space,

00:27:50.791 --> 00:27:52.790
so they just recorded
whatever was happening.

00:27:52.791 --> 00:27:54.420
So, somebody is walking
down a corridor,

00:27:54.421 --> 00:27:55.920
you can hear elevator panes,

00:27:55.921 --> 00:27:58.060
you can hear
a door slam somewhere.

00:27:58.061 --> 00:28:01.420
So, you have some transient
components in ambient noise,

00:28:01.421 --> 00:28:04.540
and so the idea was that
we generate something

00:28:04.541 --> 00:28:08.020
that's similar to that type
of ambient noise,

00:28:08.021 --> 00:28:10.310
but once you have
these transients in your signal,

00:28:10.311 --> 00:28:11.880
you should convolve it with

00:28:11.881 --> 00:28:16.575
a impulse response
from the same room,

00:28:16.576 --> 00:28:24.985
otherwise you have an echoic
noise mixed into the room.

00:28:24.986 --> 00:28:26.380
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] , 60 of the room,

00:28:26.381 --> 00:28:28.380
where the noise you're
being recorded in

00:28:28.381 --> 00:28:30.855
quantity to the noise.

00:28:30.856 --> 00:28:33.575
&gt;&gt; Yeah. The noise was similar.

00:28:33.576 --> 00:28:38.885
So, what we did is we had
shaped colored noise,

00:28:38.886 --> 00:28:40.550
Gaussian noise, call it Gaussian

00:28:40.551 --> 00:28:42.100
noise where the color spectrum

00:28:42.101 --> 00:28:45.200
is derived from some of
the noise recording.

00:28:45.201 --> 00:28:47.470
&gt;&gt; So, it's a dry noise.

00:28:47.471 --> 00:28:49.210
&gt;&gt; Dry noise. Then, added to that

00:28:49.211 --> 00:28:53.710
some an echoic random
impulsive sounds

00:28:53.711 --> 00:28:56.270
like office equipment,

00:28:56.271 --> 00:28:58.305
stuff for footsteps,
things like that.

00:28:58.306 --> 00:28:59.560
Then, take that, and

00:28:59.561 --> 00:29:01.010
convolve it with the
impulse response,

00:29:01.011 --> 00:29:04.160
so that its transient components
are convolved with this.

00:29:04.161 --> 00:29:05.180
&gt;&gt; This [inaudible].

00:29:05.181 --> 00:29:06.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:10.375
&gt;&gt; It's a compromise,
but the thing is

00:29:10.376 --> 00:29:13.625
some of the datasets only
had one impulse response,

00:29:13.626 --> 00:29:15.860
and some did have two,

00:29:15.861 --> 00:29:18.140
but we couldn't afford to
discard things just because.

00:29:18.141 --> 00:29:21.225
When there were more impulse
responses available,

00:29:21.226 --> 00:29:22.470
we used more than just

00:29:22.471 --> 00:29:25.845
one to generate noise
that we used up to four,

00:29:25.846 --> 00:29:28.880
but not many of those recordings

00:29:28.881 --> 00:29:31.915
have more than four or
five impulse responses.

00:29:31.916 --> 00:29:34.915
So, it's compromised.

00:29:34.916 --> 00:29:42.240
&gt;&gt; So, after this process,

00:29:42.241 --> 00:29:44.200
we ended up with a big corpus

00:29:44.201 --> 00:29:46.180
of data that we needed to split.

00:29:46.181 --> 00:29:49.280
So, we decided to
split the development

00:29:49.281 --> 00:29:52.925
and evaluation set
using unique Room IDs.

00:29:52.926 --> 00:29:55.985
So, by keeping track of

00:29:55.986 --> 00:29:59.570
the room where every
single belong to,

00:29:59.571 --> 00:30:03.895
we managed to separate
based on that,

00:30:03.896 --> 00:30:06.765
the training set
from the test set,

00:30:06.766 --> 00:30:09.180
so that we make sure that
the rooms present in

00:30:09.181 --> 00:30:12.790
the test set do not appear at
all in the training stage.

00:30:12.791 --> 00:30:20.585
So, the tests comprises the
whole ACE challenge datasets,

00:30:20.586 --> 00:30:25.690
which is good to
make resource more

00:30:25.691 --> 00:30:33.080
comparable with
previous ACE based papers.

