00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:03.480
So, Feature Extraction.

00:54:03.481 --> 00:54:05.730
There's a lot of features that

00:54:05.731 --> 00:54:08.730
we could explore and look better,

00:54:08.731 --> 00:54:11.310
for example the phase spectrum
we talked about,

00:54:11.311 --> 00:54:13.990
also like having some kind
of spectrogram of

00:54:13.991 --> 00:54:16.975
how does the incoming
phase behaves,

00:54:16.976 --> 00:54:21.960
some echo density profiles,
multi-channel features.

00:54:21.961 --> 00:54:25.080
We're still not
entirely clear how

00:54:25.081 --> 00:54:26.760
the room information
can be isolated

00:54:26.761 --> 00:54:28.650
from other factors,
because of course,

00:54:28.651 --> 00:54:30.650
you have absorption rates,

00:54:30.651 --> 00:54:35.755
you have different degrees
of noisy environments.

00:54:35.756 --> 00:54:37.590
Our noisy environments
in the end of the day,

00:54:37.591 --> 00:54:41.000
they're kind of a
similar settings.

00:54:41.001 --> 00:54:42.485
So, you don't have like

00:54:42.486 --> 00:54:45.250
multiple noise sources
at different distances,

00:54:45.251 --> 00:54:47.015
maybe if you had one just next to

00:54:47.016 --> 00:54:50.175
your microphone, then
everything breaks.

00:54:50.176 --> 00:54:54.700
So, this information is
not really available in

00:54:54.701 --> 00:54:56.710
literature right now and it's
something that should be

00:54:56.711 --> 00:54:59.805
more explored by having
simple experiments.

00:54:59.806 --> 00:55:02.750
The Network Model.

00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:09.370
So, I did not talk about
this very much earlier,

00:55:09.371 --> 00:55:12.735
but having a stacked
features with

00:55:12.736 --> 00:55:15.945
different unrelated features that

00:55:15.946 --> 00:55:17.630
should not be convolve together,

00:55:17.631 --> 00:55:19.480
meant that all our
convolution happening

00:55:19.481 --> 00:55:21.975
in one dimension and
not in two dimensions.

00:55:21.976 --> 00:55:24.010
So, maybe perhaps there could be

00:55:24.011 --> 00:55:25.790
some kind of hybrid network that

00:55:25.791 --> 00:55:28.500
could perform a 2D convolution

00:55:28.501 --> 00:55:30.155
just on the gammatone part,

00:55:30.156 --> 00:55:31.890
and the 1D on the others.

00:55:31.891 --> 00:55:33.915
I don't know whether
that's possible or not.

00:55:33.916 --> 00:55:36.515
Too much experience with

00:55:36.516 --> 00:55:40.570
this sort of problem but
it's something to look into.

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:43.900
Maybe the loss function

00:55:43.901 --> 00:55:46.585
can be changed
within the network,

00:55:46.586 --> 00:55:49.935
to include some form of
perceptual tolerance of

00:55:49.936 --> 00:55:56.390
how bad should the estimator

00:55:56.391 --> 00:55:59.170
labeled B compared
to the real volume.

00:55:59.171 --> 00:56:02.950
So, we don't know yet what's
our tolerance and how

00:56:02.951 --> 00:56:07.650
important it is that we are
close to the real value.

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:11.230
Of course, we can always
explore and change

00:56:11.231 --> 00:56:14.665
some of the settings
within our current models.

00:56:14.666 --> 00:56:18.340
So, Further Discussion
is of course,

00:56:18.341 --> 00:56:20.990
how accurate do we need to be
in estimating room volumes.

00:56:20.991 --> 00:56:22.840
There's been no perceptual
studies that looked at

00:56:22.841 --> 00:56:26.895
the room volume
discrimination, GNDs.

00:56:26.896 --> 00:56:31.230
Well, there has been some
studies for discrimination in

00:56:31.231 --> 00:56:35.200
terms of classification but
not in terms of regression.

00:56:35.201 --> 00:56:37.130
So, for example, what if we

00:56:37.131 --> 00:56:39.710
have a room with

00:56:39.711 --> 00:56:42.990
the same absorption coefficient
but different sizes,

00:56:42.991 --> 00:56:44.640
what is the GND that we can

00:56:44.641 --> 00:56:46.290
discriminate the size
of this room.

00:56:46.291 --> 00:56:51.420
This lack of a dataset table

00:56:51.421 --> 00:56:54.020
to isolate this parameter from
other elements right now.

00:56:54.021 --> 00:56:59.380
So, this will be a good place
to research to try to

00:56:59.381 --> 00:57:01.220
understand how important is

00:57:01.221 --> 00:57:05.185
perceptually the estimation
of the room volume.

00:57:05.186 --> 00:57:07.350
If it's used to just
compute all your parameters

00:57:07.351 --> 00:57:10.040
then some correlation
should be drawn

00:57:10.041 --> 00:57:16.385
between the parameters
estimated using the volume,

00:57:16.386 --> 00:57:18.460
using the estimated volume,

00:57:18.461 --> 00:57:22.765
and other forms of estimating
the same parameter.

00:57:22.766 --> 00:57:25.315
So, that could tell you
whether it's worth to

00:57:25.316 --> 00:57:27.740
use volume as a proxy
for, for example,

00:57:27.741 --> 00:57:30.090
the critical distance
or just rethink

00:57:30.091 --> 00:57:31.320
the whole thing and just try

00:57:31.321 --> 00:57:33.550
to estimate
the critical distance.

00:57:35.160 --> 00:57:37.840
So, the other question
is should this

00:57:37.841 --> 00:57:40.360
be in the first place
and audio problem?

00:57:40.361 --> 00:57:43.590
Well, we know that the audience
more generalizable

00:57:43.591 --> 00:57:46.295
for different types of
situations and devices,

00:57:46.296 --> 00:57:48.150
not just for
Mixed Reality headsets

00:57:48.151 --> 00:57:50.220
but computers,
laptops, telephones,

00:57:50.221 --> 00:57:53.680
all sorts of technological device

00:57:53.681 --> 00:57:55.255
that possesses a microphone.

00:57:55.256 --> 00:57:58.390
Computer vision can, of course,

00:57:58.391 --> 00:57:59.480
help to estimate or

00:57:59.481 --> 00:58:01.720
cross-validate
the room size estimation.

00:58:01.721 --> 00:58:03.560
This is just an image
from then where you

00:58:03.561 --> 00:58:06.895
depth dataset that
is something that

00:58:06.896 --> 00:58:09.790
just throw an idea
of how image could

00:58:09.791 --> 00:58:14.655
help labeling the size of a room.

00:58:14.656 --> 00:58:18.324
However, then you start
introducing assumptions

00:58:18.325 --> 00:58:20.110
and you still have to fight with

00:58:20.111 --> 00:58:22.020
the limitations of
actual technology,

00:58:22.021 --> 00:58:24.780
like depth cameras maybe
don't go very far.

00:58:24.781 --> 00:58:28.500
There are visual obstacles
might be in your room,

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:34.880
there's another degree of error

00:58:34.881 --> 00:58:37.530
that you will have
to account for.

00:58:37.531 --> 00:58:42.105
Something that has been,

00:58:42.106 --> 00:58:45.975
somebody once explained
me this idea that,

00:58:45.976 --> 00:58:49.975
if you could have
just cloud server with

00:58:49.976 --> 00:58:51.990
the room size information of

00:58:51.991 --> 00:58:54.480
all sorts of places
that being cataloged,

00:58:54.481 --> 00:58:56.630
they're ready by
engineers and then you

00:58:56.631 --> 00:58:59.915
just download your
impulse response or your device,

00:58:59.916 --> 00:59:02.325
this could be
a different way to go.

00:59:02.326 --> 00:59:09.080
But going through
the audio approach was our goal.

00:59:09.081 --> 00:59:12.000
So, the conclusions
that we can draw from

00:59:12.001 --> 00:59:13.470
this internship is

00:59:13.471 --> 00:59:16.360
that blind room volume
estimation is a hard problem,

00:59:16.361 --> 00:59:18.420
a good solution has not been yet

00:59:18.421 --> 00:59:21.030
found but some insights
were gained.

00:59:21.031 --> 00:59:24.250
Multiple feature
representations seem to bring

00:59:24.251 --> 00:59:26.020
better results than just a

00:59:26.021 --> 00:59:29.210
single time frequency
representation.

00:59:29.930 --> 00:59:34.580
Adding simulating data did
improve accuracy and reduce

00:59:34.581 --> 00:59:40.160
the average multiple
distance error.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:45.990
So, this also as
a future outlook,

00:59:45.991 --> 00:59:49.200
having more tritone data
or having maybe

00:59:49.201 --> 00:59:52.920
the future [inaudible]
when maybe in Adam work is

00:59:52.921 --> 00:59:56.505
implemented together could even

00:59:56.506 --> 01:00:02.480
bring more accurate simulations
to improve our dataset.

01:00:03.270 --> 01:00:05.335
Still some work at,

01:00:05.336 --> 01:00:07.120
but to our knowledge,

01:00:07.121 --> 01:00:08.825
this is the first
regression model

01:00:08.826 --> 01:00:11.115
for the blind estimation
of room volume.

01:00:11.116 --> 01:00:14.375
So, there was no baseline
to compare against,

01:00:14.376 --> 01:00:19.385
and there's like a big space
to move in sideways.

01:00:19.386 --> 01:00:22.270
So, that's something positive.

01:00:22.271 --> 01:00:25.070
Similar framework as
the ACE challenge

01:00:25.071 --> 01:00:26.630
makes the resource
more comparable for

01:00:26.631 --> 01:00:31.300
future attempts and
more for literature,

01:00:31.301 --> 01:00:36.710
and the community is a
little more attractive.

01:00:36.930 --> 01:00:40.155
