01:01:07.800 --> 01:01:10.925
&gt;&gt; Thanks for
the nice presentation.

01:01:10.926 --> 01:01:15.365
One comment on the features.

01:01:15.366 --> 01:01:19.445
It was one of the last slides
where you mentioned

01:01:19.446 --> 01:01:24.500
echo density would be
in a good feature.

01:01:24.501 --> 01:01:27.340
So, I think this is what mainly

01:01:27.341 --> 01:01:30.960
characterizes
the ideal room volume

01:01:30.961 --> 01:01:32.600
because it showed that you can

01:01:32.601 --> 01:01:34.060
have the same reverberation time,

01:01:34.061 --> 01:01:36.005
but different room volumes.

01:01:36.006 --> 01:01:37.895
In a large room, you will have

01:01:37.896 --> 01:01:42.130
a much less density effect
that wasn't in a small room.

01:01:42.131 --> 01:01:45.210
So, but I think you can
also determine this from

01:01:45.211 --> 01:01:49.180
the frequency response because
you have more room also,

01:01:49.181 --> 01:01:52.475
more dips and if you have
a higher echo density.

01:01:52.476 --> 01:01:54.425
So, this is probably

01:01:54.426 --> 01:01:57.775
the strongest feature
that you can use.

01:01:57.776 --> 01:02:02.980
As idea for future works
would be probably,

01:02:02.981 --> 01:02:05.255
so we saw you can

01:02:05.256 --> 01:02:07.810
somehow estimate the
reverberation time.

01:02:07.811 --> 01:02:09.460
So, it would probably
make sense to

01:02:09.461 --> 01:02:12.390
jointly estimate reverberation
time and room volume.

01:02:12.391 --> 01:02:16.120
Then, could go maybe even
further and also just to have

01:02:16.121 --> 01:02:19.280
three or four outputs
to even then estimate

01:02:19.281 --> 01:02:23.380
the DRR or probably
the source distance.

01:02:23.381 --> 01:02:25.960
But, you got probably
three, four parameters.

01:02:25.961 --> 01:02:28.625
If you can manage
them all together,

01:02:28.626 --> 01:02:30.890
then probably you have
also less confusions

01:02:30.891 --> 01:02:33.130
between room volume and
reverberation time,

01:02:33.131 --> 01:02:36.010
or something like this,
and would really give

01:02:36.011 --> 01:02:39.580
a nice characterization
of the acoustic things.

01:02:39.581 --> 01:02:44.200
It would be a nice thing,
if that would be possible.

01:02:44.201 --> 01:02:46.589
&gt;&gt; Maybe one comment
on the echo density.

01:02:46.590 --> 01:02:48.490
Unfortunately, we can't calculate

01:02:48.491 --> 01:02:50.815
that from speech signals.

01:02:50.816 --> 01:02:52.900
So, for impulse responses,

01:02:52.901 --> 01:02:56.080
that would be great, but
not for speech signal.

01:02:56.081 --> 01:02:58.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but you don't need to

01:02:58.331 --> 01:03:00.955
calculate specifically
the echo density.

01:03:00.956 --> 01:03:03.560
But, for example,
you can see this

01:03:03.561 --> 01:03:06.865
in the frequency response
of the impulse response,

01:03:06.866 --> 01:03:09.390
and we'll have more dips

01:03:09.391 --> 01:03:12.565
and more modes if we
go higher density.

01:03:12.566 --> 01:03:14.590
&gt;&gt; Yes. Maybe to turn
the question around.

01:03:14.591 --> 01:03:16.205
Maybe echo density
could be one of

01:03:16.206 --> 01:03:17.940
the outputs of the model.

01:03:17.941 --> 01:03:20.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for example.
Yeah. No doubt.

01:03:20.391 --> 01:03:23.080
&gt;&gt; So, in general, this is
called multi-skill training.

01:03:23.081 --> 01:03:25.615
Can not be forced your network
to estimate stuff,

01:03:25.616 --> 01:03:28.825
which you may not even need.

01:03:28.826 --> 01:03:31.150
This helps the network
to train better.

01:03:31.151 --> 01:03:33.220
So, I guess having

01:03:33.221 --> 01:03:36.700
additional reverberation
parameters on the output can

01:03:36.701 --> 01:03:40.195
improve the room volume

01:03:40.196 --> 01:03:44.115
itself even if you
don't use the others.

01:03:44.116 --> 01:03:47.140
&gt;&gt; Or really the idea
of idea of looking

01:03:47.141 --> 01:03:49.690
at 500 first hertz or

01:03:49.691 --> 01:03:54.400
lowest hertz and taking
the [inaudible] ,

01:03:54.401 --> 01:03:57.885
like what you said,
the echo density breaks

01:03:57.886 --> 01:03:59.950
or how do those echos come in,

01:03:59.951 --> 01:04:01.705
they kind of changed step.

01:04:01.706 --> 01:04:07.940
There's the response, then
you see it in that cepstrum.

01:04:12.300 --> 01:04:16.145
&gt;&gt; As if they are low-hanging
fruit to implement.

01:04:16.146 --> 01:04:18.205
Just try and make,

01:04:18.206 --> 01:04:20.800
even just try on

01:04:20.801 --> 01:04:22.210
impulse responses and see whether

01:04:22.211 --> 01:04:25.520
we should follow
this idea further.

01:04:25.560 --> 01:04:29.360
&gt;&gt; So, average cepstrum may
not be that much useful,

01:04:29.361 --> 01:04:31.650
but you should plot

01:04:31.660 --> 01:04:34.850
the magnitude of
this function of the time.

01:04:34.851 --> 01:04:37.175
So, cepstrum is against time.

01:04:37.176 --> 01:04:42.930
The frame, it may give
some information.

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:46.245
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Speech signals
definitely will be

01:04:46.246 --> 01:04:48.440
better than just
testing those cepstrum.

01:04:48.441 --> 01:04:50.990
These are on impulses response.

01:04:50.991 --> 01:04:54.925
So, maybe it's okay

01:04:54.926 --> 01:04:58.280
to average it on
impulse response, but, yeah.

01:04:58.281 --> 01:05:00.905
&gt;&gt; It was classic application of

01:05:00.906 --> 01:05:03.710
this cepstrum analysis
at least in

01:05:03.711 --> 01:05:10.730
the speech frontend is you do
averaging across the time,

01:05:10.731 --> 01:05:15.115
which presumably ease
the cepstrum filter

01:05:15.116 --> 01:05:16.760
and you subtract it,

01:05:16.761 --> 01:05:18.810
and what's left is the pure Sigma

01:05:18.811 --> 01:05:21.275
means removing the
impulse response

01:05:21.276 --> 01:05:26.455
between the source
and the microphone,

01:05:26.456 --> 01:05:28.350
assuming that the speech has

01:05:28.351 --> 01:05:30.955
basically a zero
average cepstrum,

01:05:30.956 --> 01:05:34.430
and then the only thing remains
is the impulse response.

01:05:34.431 --> 01:05:37.430
That mean can be
eventually useful

01:05:37.431 --> 01:05:39.435
because this is in general

01:05:39.436 --> 01:05:42.840
the cepstrum of the
impulse response.

